    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d266">

                <pb facs="0718" n="688" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v08/pg688"/>

                <head>266. Letter From Secretary of the Treasury Dillon to Secretary of Commerce Hodges<anchor n="1" xml:id="d266-f1"
                    />
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1964-12-04">December 4, 1964</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">Dear Luther:</p>
                <p>Your letter of December 2, 1964, asks where we stand on the Canadian auto parts
                        question.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d266-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>In a meeting held in George Ball’s office
                    on Monday, November 23, which you were unable to attend, there was full
                    agreement that countervailing duties should be imposed in early January if the
                    problem arising from the Canadian rebate plan has not been solved before that
                        time.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d266-f3"/>
                    <pb facs="0719" n="689" xml:id="pg689"/> Before taking such action, Treasury
                    would, of course, advise the White House and the Departments of State and
                    Commerce.</p>
                <p>With best wishes,</p>
                <p>Sincerely,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        
                            <hi rendition="#bold">Douglas</hi>
                        
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d266-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d266-f1-content">
